\fs24 \cf0 Two-Person Slide Script\
Slide 1 - Title and final scope\
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Speaker 1:
\f1\b0  Good morning. Our project is microscopy image classification using machine learning and deep learning. We classify COMI images into three cellular structures: actin, mitochondria, and nucleus. Since the midterm, we added a Baseline CNN and a transfer-learning ResNet18 model, then compared those against our Random Forest + PCA baseline.\
Slide 2 - Objective\

\f0\b Speaker 1:
\f1\b0  The goal is to test which approach recognizes cellular structure patterns most reliably. We are not only looking for high accuracy; we also want a fair evaluation method and interpretable evidence about model behavior.\
Slide 3 - Dataset\

\f0\b Speaker 1:
\f1\b0  The dataset contains fluorescence microscopy images from BPAE cells. Each image belongs to one of three classes. Actin shows the cell skeleton, mitochondria show energy-related organelles, and nucleus shows the DNA-containing center of the cell.\
Slide 4 - Grouped split strategy\

\f0\b Speaker 1:
\f1\b0  A key decision was splitting by Z group instead of randomly mixing individual images. We trained on Z004 through Z008, validated on Z009, and tested on Z010. This prevents images from the same acquisition group from leaking across train and test sets.\
Slide 5 - Model ladder\

\f0\b Speaker 1:
\f1\b0  We compare three levels of modeling. First is Random Forest + PCA, which uses flattened image features and dimensionality reduction. Second is a Baseline CNN trained from scratch. Third is ResNet18, a pretrained deep network adapted to our three classes.\
Slide 6 - Preprocessing\

\f0\b Speaker 1:
\f1\b0  All models need consistent preprocessing so the comparison is fair. Images are resized and normalized. For deep learning, we use data augmentation only during training, such as flips or small transformations, to improve generalization.\
Slide 7 - Random Forest + PCA recap\

\f0\b Speaker 1:
\f1\b0  Our midterm model was Random Forest + PCA. PCA compresses each image into fewer numerical features, and Random Forest classifies those features using many decision trees. This model performed very strongly, reaching 95.96% accuracy on the held-out test set.\
Slide 8 - Baseline CNN method\

\f0\b Speaker 2:
\f1\b0  The Baseline CNN is the first deep learning model. It learns filters directly from the images instead of using manually flattened features. Convolution layers detect local image patterns, pooling reduces spatial size, and fully connected layers output the final class probabilities.\
Slide 9 - Baseline CNN results\

\f0\b Speaker 2:
\f1\b0  The Baseline CNN reached 76.77% accuracy and 76.52% macro F1. It learned useful patterns, but it made many mistakes between mitochondria and nucleus. The largest error was 38 mitochondria images predicted as nucleus.\
Slide 10 - ResNet18 method\

\f0\b Speaker 2:
\f1\b0  ResNet18 is a deeper convolutional neural network with residual connections. We use transfer learning, meaning the model starts from features learned on a large image dataset and then we fine-tune it for our microscopy classes.\
Slide 11 - ResNet18 training\

\f0\b Speaker 2:
\f1\b0  For ResNet18, the final classification layer is replaced with a three-class output layer. The model is trained using the same train, validation, and test split. Validation performance helps select the best checkpoint before evaluating once on the held-out test set.\
Slide 12 - ResNet18 results\

\f0\b Speaker 2:
\f1\b0  ResNet18 reached 95.62% accuracy and 95.58% macro F1, which is close to the Random Forest + PCA result. It classified all nucleus test images correctly and made only 13 total errors.\
Slide 13 - Final model comparison\

\f0\b Speaker 2:
\f1\b0  The final comparison shows that Random Forest + PCA is slightly highest on this test split, but ResNet18 is nearly equal. The difference is about one image on the 297-image test set, so we should not overstate it. The main lesson is that transfer learning closed the gap dramatically compared with the CNN trained from scratch.\
Slide 14 - Error analysis\

\f0\b Speaker 2:
\f1\b0  The error patterns explain the numbers. The Baseline CNN confused mitochondria with nucleus often. ResNet18 mostly reduced those errors, while Random Forest + PCA stayed very stable across all three classes. Nucleus was the easiest class overall because its visual structure is more distinct.\
Slide 15 - Grad-CAM interpretability\

\f0\b Speaker 2:
\f1\b0  Grad-CAM helps us inspect what ResNet18 is using for a decision. It highlights important image regions for a predicted class. This does not prove the model is perfect, but it helps verify that the deep model is focusing on meaningful cellular regions rather than random background.\
Slide 16 - Limitations and future work\

\f0\b Speaker 1:
\f1\b0  The main limitation is dataset size. We used a careful grouped split, but more independent images would make the results stronger. Future work could test more architectures, tune hyperparameters, add stronger augmentation, and validate on a fully separate microscopy dataset.\
Slide 17 - Closing\

\f0\b Speaker 2:
\f1\b0  To conclude, our final project moved from a classical ML baseline to two deep learning models. The Baseline CNN showed that training from scratch is harder on a small dataset. ResNet18 showed the value of transfer learning, reaching near-best performance while giving us interpretable visual evidence through Grad-CAM.\
\
